## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and this round stays within the allowed server/test files. Input-processor and blocked-sampling lifecycle evidence are improved, but code-level EOF ordering and several required acceptance boundaries remain unresolved.

## Findings

### High — EOF handling races ahead of queued input processing

`onData` only offers chunks to the bounded queue, while the Node `end` callback calls `framer.end()` directly and immediately destroys the socket. The Effect input fiber may not yet have run `framer.push()` for preceding chunks; natural close then ends the connection scope and can interrupt that processor. Thus `framer.end()` can inspect an empty/stale buffer and complete or partial final input can be discarded. The new partial-frame test proves fibers finalize, not that the framer consumed the partial chunk before EOF validation. Enqueue an EOF marker and serialize `push`/`end` in the same connection input processor before closing.

### High — The full two-client graph still lacks exact finalization evidence

The complete replay/update/FIFO test does not assert exact finalization of both connections, four forwarders, listener close/unlink, provider event source, and provider Layer. Pre-hello, partial-frame, blocked-sample, and peer-isolation tests provide useful partial counts, but do not prove the complete production-shaped graph releases every owned resource exactly once.

### High — Executable cleanup failure remains unverified at the process boundary

Signal-handler removal and direct Layer composition are covered, but no runtime executable-path test proves signal-driven dependency-order cleanup or that close/unlink failure sets nonzero process status while retaining tagged operation/message diagnostics. Promise-facade and direct-Layer failure tests do not establish this boundary.

### Medium — Blocked-sampling exact cleanup still omits close/unlink acquisition counts

The strengthened test now covers one connected socket, listener finalization, input queue/processor, connection, sample interruption, and provider finalization. It does not count the listener close and unlink attempts or the provider source acquisition, so exact-once cleanup is still partly inferred from path absence and finalization counts.

### Medium — Actual production closing refusal remains unobserved

Calling `server.close()` at callback entry proves the enrolled-and-finalized branch because the synchronous callback continues before the Effect runtime updates `closing`. Synthetic enrollment refusal proves generic destruction, but no callback deterministically observes the production `closing` branch.

### Medium — Post-hello late-forwarder isolation remains conflated with provider teardown

The replay-forwarding test emits only after the entire provider source is disposed and does not await both forwarding-finalizer hooks. Prove a naturally disconnected post-hello client's forwarders finalize and make no write while the provider, listener, and a healthy peer remain live.

### Medium — Older integration tests still lack failure-safe cleanup

Recent lifecycle tests use `try/finally`, but many earlier socket tests release resources only on success paths. An intermediate assertion can leak sockets, listeners, or paths contrary to the package's focused-test cleanup requirement.

